From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:41:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 068/184] segacd: pause like a game does -- after every fill,
 and before every retry

Lunar's sub program, disassembled from the user's own dump, touches
exactly one hardware register directly ($FF8004 destination select)
and drives everything else through the BIOS. Its read engine ends
every run -- success or failure -- with CDC_STOP + ROM_PAUSEON, and
its stall recovery is stop, pause, wait for the BIOS to settle,
reissue, up to fifteen times.

So the pause after each fill returns as the default rather than an
experiment arm (three sources now agree: Lunar always pauses, BlastEm
shows pause is a hover with instant resume, the hardware trace shows
the drive resting in state 4), and the retry path now pauses and waits
for state 4 before reissuing the read instead of firing command 3 at a
drive still in flight. $34E6's ready list -- 1, 4, C -- was saying
this all along: a read starts from a pause.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 2d9b9da..6c993ee 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -157,7 +157,15 @@ UWORD segacd_bootflags;         /* the raw button word the servant sent.
  * CD-R left, choosing between them blind is not a decision I get to
  * make on someone else's disc. */
 static ULONG cd_lead = 20UL;
-static UBYTE cd_hold;           /* send command 6 after each fill */
+static UBYTE cd_hold = 1;       /* pause the drive after each fill.
+                                 * Default on: Lunar's sub program ends
+                                 * every read run, successful or not,
+                                 * with CDC_STOP + ROM_PAUSEON -- and
+                                 * pause is a hover, not a stop, so the
+                                 * next read starts instantly. Removing
+                                 * this on the strength of the trace was
+                                 * wrong; the pause simply happens
+                                 * outside the traced window. */
 /* When to give the decoder its second control pair.
  *
  * The BIOS writes $A0/$F8 when it resets the decoder and $A7/$F0 only
@@ -1576,8 +1584,30 @@ static BOOL cd_fetch(ULONG lba)
             cd_ring_reset();
             cdc_ints = 0;
             cdc_hit = 0;
-            if (attempt == 0)
+            if (attempt == 0) {
                 cdc_setup();
+            } else {
+                /* Pause before every reissue. This is GameArts' stall
+                 * recovery, verbatim: CDC_STOP, ROM_PAUSEON, wait for
+                 * the BIOS to settle, and only then a fresh ROM_READ --
+                 * up to fifteen times. A pause makes the drive hover on
+                 * its current track, tracking re-locked, and a read
+                 * commanded from that hover starts clean; a read
+                 * commanded at a drive still in flight is a coin toss,
+                 * and this driver had been tossing it three times and
+                 * calling the result a timeout. Wait for state 4
+                 * bounded, because $34E6's ready list -- 1, 4, C --
+                 * describes exactly what a read may start from. */
+                UBYTE base = cdd_irqs;
+
+                cdc_stop();
+                cdd_ask_ready();
+                while ((UBYTE)(cdd_irqs - base) < 40 && cd_time_left()) {
+                    if (cdd_valid && cdd_stat[0] == 0x4)
+                        break;
+                }
+                cdc_setup();
+            }
             /* No long-seek assist. The derivation of command $A stands
              * -- it is CD geometry and it is written up in
              * docs/cdd-bios-notes.md -- but the console does not use
